Both curl and wget can download files from the internet.

The main difference is their primary purpose.

=> Simple comparison

| `wget`                                           | `curl`                                                                 |
| ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Mainly designed for **downloading files**        | Mainly designed for **transferring data / communicating with servers** |
| Very convenient for downloading                  | Very powerful for APIs and HTTP requests                               |
| Automatically saves downloaded content to a file | By default, displays the response in the terminal                      |
| Good for large files and recursive downloads     | Excellent for API testing and sending requests                         |
| Simple syntax for downloads                      | More options for HTTP methods, headers, authentication, etc.           |

1. What is wget?

wget is a Linux command used to download files from the internet using the command line.

Simple way to remember:

wget = download something from a URL

For example, if you have a file available at:

https://example.com/file.txt

You can download it using:

wget https://example.com/file.txt

2. Why do we use wget?

As a Linux administrator, you may use wget to:

Download software/packages
Download scripts
Download configuration files
Download ISO files
Download files from internal web servers
Test whether a URL is accessible
Download files directly on a remote Linux server

For example, if you SSH into a server and need to download a script, you don't need a browser. You can use wget.

3. Basic Syntax
wget URL

Example:

wget https://example.com/file.txt

The file will normally be downloaded into your current directory.

You can check your current directory with:

pwd

And see the downloaded file with:

ls -l

4. Simple Lab

Let's do a safe practice lab on your Ubuntu/WSL.

Step 1: Create a lab directory
mkdir wget-lab
cd wget-lab

Check where you are:

pwd

You should see something similar to:

/home/bilal/wget-lab

Step 2: Download a test file

Run:

wget https://www.example.com/

You may see output showing that wget connected to the website and downloaded the page.

Now check:

ls -l

You should see a file such as:

index.html

Step 3: Read the downloaded file

Run:

cat index.html

You will see the HTML content of the downloaded webpage.

So the flow is:

URL
 ↓
wget
 ↓
Download
 ↓
File saved in current directory

5. Download and Give the File a Different Name

Suppose you want to save the downloaded file with a specific name.

Use:

wget -O myfile.html https://www.example.com/

Here:

-O = output filename

Check:

ls -l

You should see:

myfile.html

6. Download to a Specific Directory

You can tell wget where to save the file.

Example:

wget -P /tmp https://www.example.com/

Here:

-P /tmp

means:

Save the downloaded file inside /tmp.

Check:

ls -l /tmp

7. See Download Progress

Normally wget already shows download progress:

Downloading...
[=======>........] 45%

This is useful when downloading large files such as ISO images.

8. Resume an Interrupted Download

One very useful option is:

wget -c URL

-c means continue.

For example:

wget -c https://example.com/bigfile.iso

If the download gets interrupted, wget -c can continue from where it stopped, when the server supports resuming.

9. Download in the Background

You can use:

wget -b URL

-b means background.

Example:

wget -b https://example.com/bigfile.iso

This is useful when downloading a large file and you don't want the terminal to remain occupied.

10. Check if wget is Installed

Run:

wget --version

If it is installed, you will see version information.

If you get:

wget: command not found

On Ubuntu/Debian, install it with:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install wget
